1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,762
A foreign tourist in Kathmandu, warmed by whisky, tells his Nepali guide how wonderful the country is: its peace, its gods, its carved windows, its smiles, and everywhere, eyes.

2
00:00:12,762 --> 00:00:16,551
Tomorrow, he says, show me beautiful eyes.

3
00:00:16,551 --> 00:00:21,997
Take me to a lonely stupa to watch the sunset reflected in the eyes of the Buddha.

4
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,027
The next day the guide does show him a pair of eyes.

5
00:00:25,027 --> 00:00:26,272
But they are not the Buddha's.

6
00:00:31,767 --> 00:00:40,984
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 6: The Half closed Eyes of the Buddha and the Slowly Sinking Sun, by Shankar Lamichhane.

7
00:00:43,367 --> 00:00:44,462
Six parts.

8
00:00:44,462 --> 00:00:46,077
Shankar Lamichhane.

9
00:00:46,077 --> 00:00:47,509
The tourist's evening.

10
00:00:47,509 --> 00:00:48,603
The guide's day.

11
00:00:48,603 --> 00:00:51,259
Two monologues making one picture.

12
00:00:51,259 --> 00:00:52,431
The themes.

13
00:00:52,431 --> 00:00:54,103
And the irony of the eyes.

14
00:00:58,467 --> 00:01:05,357
Shankar Lamichhane was born in Kathmandu in nineteen twenty eight and raised by his mother in Banaras.

15
00:01:05,357 --> 00:01:17,023
He studied science, worked for government and cultural bodies, and later managed a handicrafts store, where he met many foreign visitors, exactly like the tourist in this story.

16
00:01:17,023 --> 00:01:25,318
He is one of Nepal's best loved essayists, and his stories are lyrical and full of symbols, with little conventional plot.

17
00:01:25,318 --> 00:01:30,545
This one appeared in nineteen sixty two, and you read it in Michael Hutt's English translation.

18
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:34,060
The story has an unusual shape.

19
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:37,094
There is no narrator and almost no action.

20
00:01:37,094 --> 00:01:43,214
Part one is the tourist's speech, the evening before, over drinks; the guide never answers.

21
00:01:43,214 --> 00:01:48,188
Part two is the guide's speech the next day; now the tourist never answers.

22
00:01:48,188 --> 00:01:50,822
What joins them is the image of eyes.

23
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:56,422
Part one.

24
00:01:56,422 --> 00:01:58,974
The tourist praises everything.

25
00:01:58,974 --> 00:02:04,859
The view from the plane: neat fields, coloured houses, an age old peace.

26
00:02:04,859 --> 00:02:13,583
The East, he says, has only ever given to the West, religion, art, civilisation, without any idea of ownership.

27
00:02:13,583 --> 00:02:21,526
He knows the history from books: the valley as a lake, Manjushri's sword, the monks' pure samyak gaze.

28
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:31,797
He admires the carved houses, the harmony of Hindus and Buddhists, the momos, an old man telling his grandson stories.

29
00:02:31,797 --> 00:02:37,539
And above all the smile, and the eyes: carved windows, stupas, people.

30
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:40,137
Nepal, he says, is a land of eyes.

31
00:02:40,367 --> 00:02:46,931
And notice how his speech is punctuated by drinks: one more drink before dinner, cheers once again.

32
00:02:46,931 --> 00:02:49,900
The warmer the whisky, the warmer the praise.

33
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:53,181
He ends: show me beautiful, full eyes.

34
00:02:53,181 --> 00:02:54,674
Come, let's go to eat dinner.

35
00:02:59,467 --> 00:03:00,354
Part two.

36
00:03:00,354 --> 00:03:04,820
Come, my guest, says the guide; today I am to show you some eyes.

37
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:20,992
He takes the tourist up Chobhar hill, past the gorge of the Manjushri legend, past a village artist's drawing of birds and a temple of no named religion, to the Adinath temple, where Shiva and the Buddha stand together and children play.

38
00:03:20,992 --> 00:03:23,479
The tourist would call it living tolerance.

39
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:26,083
The guide agrees, but will not stop there.

40
00:03:26,083 --> 00:03:31,430
He climbs on, to a farmer's family who pay half their harvest to a landlord in the city.

41
00:03:32,633 --> 00:03:34,848
In the house is a child with polio.

42
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:39,366
He cannot speak, move his hands, chew, or even spit.

43
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:41,189
Only his eyes are alive.

44
00:03:41,189 --> 00:03:45,213
The tourist said the East is always giving to the West.

45
00:03:45,213 --> 00:03:50,551
Here, says the guide, is a child who can neither give nor take anything at all.

46
00:03:50,551 --> 00:03:55,174
And he confesses: he told the parents the tourist is a doctor.

47
00:03:55,174 --> 00:04:02,921
Now they smile at him with faith, as if he were their eldest son, home with a cure from across the seven seas.

48
00:04:02,921 --> 00:04:05,879
Every one of the tourist's phrases, turned inside out.

49
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:11,737
The boy has a little sister who crawls about, falls, knocks things over.

50
00:04:11,737 --> 00:04:16,711
For a moment his eyes want to copy her, then sink back into indifference.

51
00:04:16,711 --> 00:04:31,242
When their mother scolds her, a light gleams in his eyes, and the guide imagines what he would say: how sweet it would be to fall and scrape a knee, to cry, to break something and feel guilty, to learn that fire burns.

52
00:04:31,242 --> 00:04:35,031
In the sister, words carry a whole culture forward.

53
00:04:35,031 --> 00:04:37,793
In him, the chain of human development ends.

54
00:04:42,567 --> 00:04:44,782
Put the two speeches side by side.

55
00:04:44,782 --> 00:04:48,793
Evening, over drinks, against the next day, sober.

56
00:04:48,793 --> 00:04:54,053
The East that only gives, against a child who can neither give nor take.

57
00:04:54,053 --> 00:04:58,207
Peace and smiles, against poverty and helplessness.

58
00:04:58,207 --> 00:05:01,983
History from books, against the pulse of reality.

59
00:05:01,983 --> 00:05:05,597
The Buddha's eyes, against the eyes of a paralysed boy.

60
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:07,895
The two men.

61
00:05:07,895 --> 00:05:13,312
The tourist is not a villain; he is generous and wants to love the place.

62
00:05:13,312 --> 00:05:22,622
But he sees only what he came expecting, and turns living people into a postcard: his fault is sentimentality, which is a kind of blindness.

63
00:05:22,622 --> 00:05:26,541
The guide is ironic, patient and quietly angry.

64
00:05:26,541 --> 00:05:35,356
And he is morally complicated: he lies to the parents, and uses the boy to teach a lesson, while insisting the boy is not a symbol.

65
00:05:35,356 --> 00:05:37,643
That unease is part of the story's honesty.

66
00:05:42,433 --> 00:05:43,411
Themes.

67
00:05:43,411 --> 00:05:54,622
Seeing and not seeing: the samyak gaze, the Buddhist ideal of seeing things as they are, belongs not to the tourist who claims it, but to whoever looks at the whole picture.

68
00:05:54,622 --> 00:06:02,096
East and West: the claim that the East only gives is flattering, but it turns real people into a type.

69
00:06:02,096 --> 00:06:04,544
Critics call this habit Orientalism.

70
00:06:04,544 --> 00:06:06,523
Poverty behind the postcard.

71
00:06:06,523 --> 00:06:12,390
And harmony: the guide accepts that Nepal's tolerance is real, but refuses to stop there.

72
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:23,719
Orientalism is the critic Edward Said's word, from nineteen seventy eight, for imagining Asia as exotic, spiritual, timeless and passive.

73
00:06:23,719 --> 00:06:28,573
Lamichhane's story criticises that habit sixteen years before the word existed.

74
00:06:33,367 --> 00:06:44,111
The guide ends by describing the boy's eyes in the tourist's own language: eyes surrounded by mountains, eyes that welcome, eyes in which hides the end of life.

75
00:06:44,111 --> 00:06:46,481
And then the final sentence: Look!

76
00:06:46,481 --> 00:06:52,470
They are just as beautiful as the setting sun's reflection in the eyes of the Buddha.

77
00:06:52,470 --> 00:06:55,913
Beauty and suffering, joined into one picture.

78
00:06:56,133 --> 00:07:01,043
It is bitter irony: he does not mean beautiful the way the tourist meant it.

79
00:07:01,043 --> 00:07:08,023
And at the same time it is a demand that the tourist look at the whole of Nepal, the Buddha and the boy together.

80
00:07:08,023 --> 00:07:11,937
The slowly sinking sun of the title is that ending of life.

81
00:07:13,167 --> 00:07:14,627
Devices to quote.

82
00:07:14,627 --> 00:07:24,965
Irony: the guide repeats the tourist's own praise, that only an Easterner could endure such a fate without complaint, and turns it into an accusation.

83
00:07:24,965 --> 00:07:30,499
Symbols: the Buddha's eyes, the boy's eyes, the setting sun.

84
00:07:30,499 --> 00:07:32,973
Contrast in every pairing.

85
00:07:32,973 --> 00:07:34,796
Two dramatic monologues.

86
00:07:34,796 --> 00:07:36,385
The motif of eyes.

87
00:07:36,385 --> 00:07:39,587
And imagery that turns the land into a face.

88
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:42,770
Pause and think.

89
00:07:50,067 --> 00:07:54,753
The tourist speaks in Part One, the guide in Part Two; neither listener answers.

90
00:07:56,733 --> 00:07:57,168
One more.

91
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:11,321
He is kind but sees only the romantic picture he expected — the poverty is invisible to him.

92
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:14,595
Quick recap.

93
00:08:14,595 --> 00:08:17,759
Lamichhane, and a story from nineteen sixty two.

94
00:08:17,759 --> 00:08:20,272
A tourist's whisky-warm praise.

95
00:08:20,272 --> 00:08:24,140
A guide's sober reply: a boy who can neither give nor take.

96
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:27,590
Seeing against not seeing, East against West.

97
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:29,458
And the bitter irony of the eyes.

98
00:08:33,633 --> 00:08:39,194
That was The Half closed Eyes of the Buddha and the Slowly Sinking Sun.

99
00:08:39,194 --> 00:08:46,486
Next, an old man with enormous wings falls into a muddy courtyard: Gabriel García Márquez.

100
00:08:46,486 --> 00:08:47,057
See you there.

